  "title": "Printing Only the Required Number of Digits",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Course: Learn C Programming"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Printing Only the Required Number of Digits"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If we have a double variable PI assigned a value of 3.1415 and we print it, we'll get 3.141500 as the output."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "This is because printf() prints 6 digits after the decimal point by default."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "To restrict printf() to print 2 digits after the decimal point, we can use the format specifier %.2lf."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Similarly, we can use %.3lf, %.4lf, and so on to print 3, 4 and more digits respectively."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's see how we can improve our previous program to print exactly four digits of our double variable."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "c",
      "content": "1#include <stdio.h>\n2\n3int main() {\n4\n5    // Initialize a double variable\n6    double PI = 3.1415;\n7    \n8     // Print six digits after the decimal point\n9     printf(\"%lf\", PI);\n10\n11    // Print a space\n12    printf(\" \");\n13\n14   // Use %.4lf for 4 digits of double variable\n15   printf(\"%.4lf\", PI);\n16\n17   return 0;\n18}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "3.141500 3.1415"
    },
